menu "Fractonica core"

    config FR_TEST_PIN
        int "T-LAT test pin (-1 = none)"
        range -1 48
        default -1
        help
            The board manifest's test_pin (spec 04 §4.3), written into sdkconfig.board by `fr gen firmware`,
            which checks that it is a free pin no instance claims. Debug builds (FR_TEST_HOOKS) drive it
            open-drain for diag `pin_pulse <us>` (design c6 D24): released at idle, low during a pulse, never
            pushed high, since the T-LAT rig wires it to another board's BOOT pin, a strapping pin that must
            read high whenever that board resets.

    config FR_ROLE
        int "HELLO role (0 node, 1 gateway, 2 fixture)"
        range 0 2
        default 0
        help
            The PRESENCE.HELLO role (schema/protocol.yaml Role, spec 05 §5.7), set from the profile's role by
            `fr gen firmware`. The HIL fixture's profile sets 2 (design m2-c1-hil D6): the hub keeps a fixture
            out of `all`, fences it from clients and never shows it to apps.

    config FR_CORE_TASK_STACK
        int "Core task stack (bytes)"
        range 8192 32768
        default 8192
        help
            The fr_core task's stack. Every dispatch runs on it, a bulk Reply (1445 B) and fr.diag exec's cJSON
            included. `fr gen firmware` raises it in sdkconfig.board for a profile with an fr.display instance
            (design m2-c5-display D3): the generated fr.display dispatch decodes a ui_load layout (24 widgets, about
            1.7 KB per copy) in nested copies before the service sees it, on any panel (a mono one answers
            UNSUPPORTED only after the decode).

    # fr.csi (design csi-c1 D10, §12): CsiService's buffers, internal RAM. A profile's `memory: small` has
    # `fr gen firmware` lower them in sdkconfig.board.

    config FR_CSI_RING_BYTES
        int "CSI record ring (bytes, a power of two)"
        range 2048 32768
        default 8192
        help
            Records on their way from the Wi-Fi task to fr_stream, allocated at init: 8 KB holds about 60 LLTF or
            16 HE20 records (0.15-0.6 s at 100 Hz). A power of two (2048, 4096, 8192, 16384, 32768; CsiService
            static_asserts it): the ring's byte counters run free and wrap at 2^32.

    config FR_CSI_BLOCK_BYTES
        int "CSI stream block buffer (bytes, each of two)"
        range 2048 8192
        default 8192
        help
            A stream block's buffer, at most csi::kBlockMaxBytes (the hub takes any size up to it). Both come from
            the heap while a stream runs and go back at its end.

    config FR_CORE_TASK_WDT
        bool "Watch the core task with the task watchdog"
        depends on ESP_TASK_WDT_INIT
        default y if ESP_TASK_WDT_PANIC
        help
            The core task subscribes to the task watchdog and feeds it from its loop, whose idle wait then lasts
            at most 1 s, so a callback that blocks (or spins) past CONFIG_ESP_TASK_WDT_TIMEOUT_S trips it, not
            only an idle task it starves. On by default exactly when a timeout panics and reboots
            (CONFIG_ESP_TASK_WDT_PANIC): the HIL fixture's fixture_gpio driver sets that (design m2-c1-hil D11
            item 1), so a wedged fixture reboots with every line let go instead of sitting offline. Off, a
            timeout only logs, as on every other profile.

endmenu
